WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.200
Privileged access has one core rule: no
standing access to privileged roles.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.700
Every privileged assignment is
eligible and activated just in time.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.600
The only permanent active assignments
are the two emergency accounts.

4
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.150
The mapping from AD groups is the
program's design, not an equivalence.

5
00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:23.235
Domain Admins maps to Global
Administrator, held by very few; Microsoft

6
00:00:23.235 --> 00:00:28.838
recommends fewer than five, and most
Domain Admin duties map to narrower roles.

7
00:00:28.838 --> 00:00:32.478
Enterprise Admins maps to
Privileged Role Administrator,

8
00:00:32.478 --> 00:00:34.187
with its own approval chain.

9
00:00:34.187 --> 00:00:38.905
Account Operators becomes User Administrator
scoped to administrative units;

10
00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:42.143
Conditional Access can't
target AU-scoped assignments,

11
00:00:42.143 --> 00:00:45.663
so put those administrators in
a group for policy targeting.

12
00:00:45.663 --> 00:00:49.602
Server Operators moves to Intune
or Azure role-based access,

13
00:00:49.602 --> 00:00:52.517
Backup Operators to
service-specific roles,

14
00:00:52.517 --> 00:00:57.638
and Group Policy Creator Owners to the
Intune Policy and Profile Manager role.

15
00:00:57.638 --> 00:01:02.353
The PIM standards: Global Administrator
and Privileged Role Administrator

16
00:01:02.353 --> 00:01:07.288
activate for at most four hours, with
approval, justification and a ticket number.

17
00:01:07.288 --> 00:01:10.562
Security Administrator gets
eight hours with approval.

18
00:01:10.562 --> 00:01:13.860
Most other admin roles get
eight hours without approval,

19
00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.474
and the reader roles twelve.

20
00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:20.637
Eligibility expires within twelve months
and renews through an access review.

21
00:01:20.637 --> 00:01:25.071
And because PIM's own MFA setting can
be satisfied by an earlier sign-in,

22
00:01:25.071 --> 00:01:29.427
the program uses PIM's authentication
context option to force fresh,

23
00:01:29.427 --> 00:01:32.713
phishing-resistant
authentication at activation.

24
00:01:32.713 --> 00:01:37.000
Configure named approvers outside
the requester's chain, because with

25
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.288
none configured, PIM falls back to
active admins and can lock the tenant.

26
00:01:41.288 --> 00:01:45.631
Emergency access: two cloud-only
accounts in the onmicrosoft domain,

27
00:01:45.631 --> 00:01:49.976
permanent active Global Administrator,
on a passkey security key or

28
00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:54.549
certificate-based authentication,
each credential stored separately.

29
00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:59.376
Microsoft's mandatory MFA for
admin portals applies to them too,

30
00:01:59.376 --> 00:02:01.925
so a password in a safe isn't enough.

31
00:02:01.925 --> 00:02:05.089
Exclude them from every
restrictive policy,

32
00:02:05.089 --> 00:02:09.500
alert on every sign-in, and test
them at least every ninety days.
